# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored — never commit real keys.
# Loaded by src/env.ts, which every CLI and playwright.config.ts imports.

# OPTIONAL, and not the route to reach for. The SDK finds its own credential: the OAuth
# session from `claude login`, or CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN below, or an apiKeyHelper, a
# managed key, Bedrock/Vertex. If you are signed in to Claude Code you need nothing here —
# verified 2026-09-13 by running a role with this variable absent from the process. This
# line used to read "Required for the agent roles", and naming it as the remedy for an auth
# failure is what caused a key to be issued and pasted in that nobody needed. Set it only to
# bill an API account instead of a subscription; the CI job authenticates with the token
# below, not with this.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# A subscription token, for a machine or a CI job with no interactive session. `claude
# setup-token` prints it; the CI job authenticates with this and nothing else. This is the
# route that works when `claude auth status` reports `loggedIn: false` — the state a fresh
# machine is in — and it is the one a person asked about on 2026-09-24 because this file
# documented the key and the login but never the token.
CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=

# Other providers. Not used by the harness today — present so a role or tool that
# needs a second opinion, or a cross-model comparison, has a documented home for
# its credentials rather than inventing one.
OPENAI_API_KEY=
GEMINI_API_KEY=
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
XAI_API_KEY=
MISTRAL_API_KEY=

# The one place the harness's model is chosen. A tier alias (haiku | sonnet | opus)
# or a full model id. The tier also scales every role's turn and spend budget —
# see src/agents/models.ts. Leave unset for sonnet.
HARNESS_MODEL=sonnet

# Base URL of the app under test. The bundled example app runs on 4173.
BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4173

# Rabbit-hole guards. Every agent run is bounded by turns, spend and wall time;
# whichever trips first ends the run with a partial result rather than an open loop.
#
# Turns and spend are deliberately NOT set here. Each role declares the turns its own
# work needs and the tier scales them; setting AGENT_MAX_TURNS overrides all of that
# for every role at once. This file used to ship 12, which cut exploratory-tester from
# the 30 it asks for to 12 — the tightest budget on the most open-ended role. Set it
# only when you mean to override, and expect the run to say that you did.
# AGENT_MAX_TURNS=
# AGENT_MAX_USD=
#
# The wall clock is the same lesson, and this file shipped 180000 — three minutes —
# while learning it for turns two lines above. Every role declares a wall clock measured
# from what its work actually takes: 600s for unit-coder, 1800s for exploratory-tester,
# 3600s for integration-coder after its first live run needed 2804s. This variable beat
# all of them at once, so integration-coder started with 280s and would have been cut off
# at a tenth of the work.
#
# It is caught only because the run says "AGENT_TIMEOUT_MS overrides the role's 3600s"
# in its opening banner. A person who does not read that line gets a starved run and a
# thin report, and nothing about the report says why.
#
# Set it when you mean to override — a quick smoke run, a hard CI ceiling — and expect
# the banner to say you did.
# AGENT_TIMEOUT_MS=
#
# The spend bound that actually acts, and the reason the dollar figure above is not one.
#
# Measured 2026-10-06 by instrumenting the SDK stream: cost arrives in a single `result`
# message at the END of a run, so for the whole of every run AGENT_MAX_USD had nothing to
# compare against. It is a post-mortem, not a cap — which is how an exploratory session spent
# an unmeasured amount against a $1.00 limit and was stopped only by the wall clock.
#
# Token usage rides on every assistant message, so a ceiling on tokens fires mid-run. Leave
# this unset and the runner derives one from whichever dollar figure is in force, at the one
# measured rate in src/agents/models.ts (484,792 weighted units per dollar, sonnet, 2026-10-07).
# The unit is an input-token-equivalent, not a token: a cache read costs a tenth of an input
# token and a write costs double, and summing them raw overstated by twentyfold and stopped a
# role before it took a single action. Set it only to bound a run directly, and expect the
# banner to say that you did.
#
# The banner prints what is actually enforced: "$1.00 as 242,362 tokens", or "NO spend bound in
# force" when there is none. A bare dollar figure used to read as a cap and was not one.
#
# A $1 tier budget buys roughly 23 messages of exploratory-tester, which is two or three
# actions. If a session keeps stopping with its work unfinished, this is the number to raise.
# AGENT_MAX_TOKENS=
